cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  svg-ambiguity-bench: measuring visual-reference resolution in
  under-determined SVG markup
message: If you use this benchmark or its data, please cite it.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: G
    given-names: Nitish R
repository-code: https://github.com/NITISH-R-G/svg-ambiguity-bench
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-07-28"
keywords:
  - benchmark
  - svg
  - large language models
  - visual grounding
  - evaluation methodology
abstract: >-
  A controlled benchmark for editing SVG markup that does not encode the visual
  properties its instructions refer to. Each generated SVG contains several
  elements sharing a tag and a fill whose path geometry is replaced by opaque
  fixed-length tokens, so position and size cannot be read from the source.
  Instructions identify exactly one element by spatial position or by ordinal
  size. A small local model is measured with no additional context, with derived
  visual facts, and with those same facts permuted between elements - the last
  isolating whether any improvement comes from the information supplied or merely
  from the enumerated format it arrives in. The primary metric is identification
  accuracy, reported per predicate, with abstention treated as a distinct outcome
  rather than a failure.
